      "text": "In rectangular coordinates we can find spherical coordinates using $\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2},$ $\\theta = \\arctan{\\left( \\frac{y}{x} \\right)},$ and $\\phi = \\arccos{\\left( \\frac{z}{\\rho} \\right)}.$\nFirst, we find $\\rho:$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\rho &= \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3 \\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{36} \\\\\n&= 6.\n\\end{align*}Next, we find $\\theta:$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\theta &= \\arctan{\\left( \\frac{y}{x} \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\arctan{\\left( \\frac{-3 \\sqrt{3}}{0} \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\pi.\n\\end{align*}Finally, we find $\\phi:$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\phi &= \\arccos{\\left( \\frac{z}{\\rho} \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\arccos{\\left( \\frac{3}{6} \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\arccos{\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n\\end{align*}Therefore, the spherical coordinates of the point $(0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3)$ are $\\boxed{(6, \\pi, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}.$The answer is: (6,\\pi,\\frac{\\pi}{3})",
