      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we need to use the following formulas:\n\n1. \\(\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\\)\n2. \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\\) (with considering the quadrant)\n3. \\(\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\\)\n\nLet's start with \\(\\rho\\):\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSince the point \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) is in the downward position of the third quadrant, we need to add \\(\\pi\\) to the angle obtained from the arctangent function:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(\\tan^{-1}(\\infty) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi + \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate \\(\\phi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThe value of \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
