      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To convert the point \\((x, y, z) = (0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates, we use the following formulas:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right).\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate each component step-by-step.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(\\rho\\)\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(\\theta\\)\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right).\n\\]\nSince \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = -3\\sqrt{3}\\), the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) lies on the negative \\(y\\)-axis. The angle \\(\\theta\\) that this angle makes with the positive \\(x\\)-axis is \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) (or \\(\\frac{3\\pi}{2}\\) if we consider the positive direction without rotation). However, since we are in the fourth quadrant where \\(y\\) is negative, we keep the negative angle:\n\\[\n\\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(\\phi\\)\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right).\n\\]\nWe know that \\(\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\).\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, -\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}.\n\\]",
